// ATLANTIS: THE ANTEDILUVIAN WORLD, BY IGNATIUS DONNELLY (NEW YORK: HARPER &
// BROTHERS, 1882). A SELECTION OF FIVE CHAPTERS.
//
// The text comes from donnelly-text.ts, generated by
// scripts/texts/atlantis-authors.py from Project Gutenberg #4032, voted word
// by word against the archive.org OCRs of four printed copies of the 1882
// setting (three 'Eleventh Edition' copies, among them Duke University's, and
// the 'Twenty-fourth Edition' of 1904, from the same plates). The majority
// reading is taken; every reading the vote took and every correction made by
// hand is listed in the script, and the doubtful places were read on the Duke
// page images. No copy of the very first printing was reached.
//
// THE SELECTION. Part I, chapters I–III (the book's thirteen propositions,
// Plato's Timaeus and Critias quoted at length in Jowett's English, and the
// case for the story's truth), with the two chapters elsewhere in the book that
// retell or quote the dialogues' story as their subject: Part II, chapter I
// (the 'great deluge of all') and Part IV, chapter II (the kings of Atlantis
// as the gods of the Greeks). The chapters that quote Plato only in passing
// are listed with the book and not imported.
//
// THE THESIS. Donnelly argues that Plato's Atlantis was a real island in the
// Atlantic and the source of the world's civilisations. Modern geology and
// archaeology reject it: there is no sunken continent under the Atlantic,
// whose floor is ocean crust formed at the Mid-Atlantic Ridge, and the
// likenesses he draws between peoples do not show descent from a lost island.
// The archive gives the argument as his, not as evidence for Atlantis. His
// date for its destruction, about 9600 BC, is Plato's figure taken as
// history and is recorded as the book's claim. His ranking of races is the
// text's own.
//
// Nothing is marked verified: the text is a careful copy, not a reading
// checked page by page against the book.

